.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-s\f[R], \f[B]\(ensocket\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
Set the Unix domain socket path explicitly.
See \f[B]SOCKET PATH RESOLUTION\f[R] below for the default behavior when
this option is omitted.
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\(endaemon\f[R]
Daemonize the process (double\-fork, setsid, redirect stdio to
/dev/null).
.TP
\f[B]\-p\f[R], \f[B]\(enpidfile\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
Write the server PID to \f[I]PATH\f[R] after startup.
The file is removed on clean shutdown.
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\(enmax\-clients\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Maximum number of simultaneous client connections.
Default: \f[B]10\f[R].
Minimum: 1.
.TP
\f[B]\-P\f[R], \f[B]\(enno\-peercred\f[R]
Disable the \f[CR]SO_PEERCRED\f[R] credential handshake on accepted
connections.
Required for compatibility with Python and other clients that do not
support the Cygwin\-specific peer credential exchange.
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\(enlogin\f[R]
Initialize each session as a login shell.
Sources \f[CR]/etc/profile\f[R], \f[CR]\(ti/.bash_profile\f[R],
\f[CR]\(ti/.bash_login\f[R], and \f[CR]\(ti/.profile\f[R] in the
standard Bash login sequence.
.TP
\f[B]\(ennorc\f[R]
Skip sourcing \f[CR]\(ti/.bashrc\f[R] during session initialization.
.TP
\f[B]\(ennoprofile\f[R]
Skip sourcing \f[CR]/etc/profile\f[R] and \f[CR]\(ti/.bash_profile\f[R]
during login shell initialization.
Only meaningful when combined with \f[B]\(enlogin\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-I\f[R], \f[B]\(eninit\f[R] \f[I]SCRIPT\f[R]
Source the file \f[I]SCRIPT\f[R] as additional initialization for each
new session, after the standard startup files.
.TP
\f[B]\-S\f[R], \f[B]\(enstdio\f[R]
Use stdin/stdout for client I/O instead of creating a Unix socket.
Operates in single\-session mode.
Useful for integration with process supervisors or SSH forced commands.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\(enfd\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Use the inherited file descriptor \f[I]N\f[R] for client I/O instead of
creating a Unix socket.
Operates in single\-session mode.
Intended for use with socket activation (e.g., systemd).
.TP
\f[B]\-A\f[R], \f[B]\(enauth\-fd\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Write the authentication token to file descriptor \f[I]N\f[R] instead of
stderr.
Only applies in \f[B]\(enstdio\f[R] and \f[B]\(enfd\f[R] modes.
Default: stderr.
.TP
\f[B]\-W\f[R], \f[B]\(ennamed\-pipe\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Listen on the Windows Named Pipe
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs.\(rspipe\(rsbash\-server\-\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R] instead of a
Unix domain socket.
Cygwin only.
Allows native Windows processes to connect to the server.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\(enverbose\f[R]
Print diagnostic messages to stderr, including client connect/disconnect
events and child process lifecycle.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\(enhelp\f[R]
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\(enversion\f[R]
Print version information and exit.
.SH SOCKET PATH RESOLUTION
When \f[B]\(ensocket\f[R] is not specified, the socket path is
determined by the first matching rule:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]\(ensocket\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R] on the command line.
.IP "2." 3
The \f[B]$BASH_SERVER_SOCKET\f[R] environment variable.
.IP "3." 3
A \f[CR]socket\f[R] directive in \f[B]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R].
.IP "4." 3
**$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR**/bash\-server/sock (if \(ga$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\(ga is
set).
.IP "5." 3
/tmp/bash\-server\-\f[I]<uid>\f[R]/sock (fallback).
.PP
The directory containing the socket is created automatically (mode 0700)
if it does not exist.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]BASH_SERVER_SOCKET\f[R]
Unix socket path.
Overridden by \f[B]\(ensocket\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\f[R]
Base directory for runtime files.
Used in socket path resolution (rule 4).
.TP
\f[B]HOME\f[R]
User home directory.
Used to locate \f[B]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R] and startup files.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R]
Optional configuration file.
One directive per line; \f[CR]#\f[R] introduces comments.
Currently recognized directives:
.RS
.IP
.EX
socket PATH
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[I]<socket\-path>\f[R]
The Unix domain socket file.
Created on startup, removed on shutdown.
Permissions: 0600.
.TP
\f[I]<socket\-path>\f[R].token
The authentication token file.
Contains the hex\-encoded 256\-bit token.
Created on startup, removed on shutdown.
Permissions: 0600.
.TP
\f[I]<pidfile>\f[R]
PID file (only when \f[B]\(enpidfile\f[R] is used).
Removed on shutdown.
